## 6. Adjacent CXC-COMP extensions captured this session

> ⛔ **NOT the generic engine, and NOT structurally part of it either.** Everything in this section is [SPEC:CXC-COMP] POLICY — SVG-slot and screen-composition material — captured HERE only because it surfaced in the same review pass that produced this document. [CXC-MENU-WHY]'s "engine sections MUST NOT reference BSG" rule does not apply here; this section is exempt by design (frontmatter `not-this`). Each node below carries `part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]` (its true semantic home) rather than `[SPEC:CXC-MENU]` — filing it under the menu-resolver spec would assert composition/rendering nodes as structural members of the resolver engine, exactly the seam-blur this document exists to avoid — plus a `captured-here` note recording that it is physically authored in THIS file pending migration. The MIGRATION ITSELF is now a RATIFIED follow-on ([DD-MENU-SPEC-HOME]), not merely a maybe; each node's OWN `[PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED]` tag still stands until that migration actually happens — the design decisions ABOUT this content (content-band, fit-mode, slot-scene-grammar) are separately ratified below and are not affected by the pending physical move.

### [CXC-MENU-CONTENT-BAND] Content-band isolation — an opt-in inverse of `ref:` for a screen SVG's renderable span

- definition: a screen SVG's renderable content is bracketed by a CHROME-AGNOSTIC sentinel PAIR — two marker layers, `content-start` and `content-end` — placed as ordinary top-level groups in SVG document order. Everything BETWEEN the pair (exclusive of the sentinels themselves) is renderable content; everything OUTSIDE the pair (before `content-start`, after `content-end`) is authoring CONTEXT, skipped by the derivation with NO per-layer `ref:` tag required. This is the OPT-IN inverse of [CXC-COMP-REF]'s opt-OUT `ref:` convention: instead of marking every context group individually, an author brackets the content ONCE and everything outside is implicitly context.
- requirement: the derivation MUST verify EXACTLY ONE well-formed, correctly-ordered `content-start`/`content-end` pair per screen SVG that uses this convention (content-start appears before content-end in document order; no more than one of each). A MISSING or malformed sentinel (zero pairs, more than one pair, out-of-order pair, an unpaired singleton) MUST fail LOUD as an ERROR — never a silent fall-back to treating the whole document as content.
- why-document-order-suffices: in SVG, document order IS paint order IS z-order — there is no separate z-index channel, so the sentinel pair's DOCUMENT-order bracket is, without any extra bookkeeping, also the LAYER-order bracket [CXC-COMP-LAYER-ORDER] already derives the render's z-order from. The band needs no coordination with that derivation; it is reading the same ordering.
- relates-to: [CXC-COMP-REF] is the existing opt-out convention this is the opt-in inverse of; [CXC-COMP-LAYER-ORDER] is the z-order derivation the `why-document-order-suffices` note ties to; this convention EXTENDS [SPEC:CXC-COMP]'s domain (screen-SVG authoring), not the generic menu-resolver engine.
- placement-status: "[PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED]. Its natural home is [SPEC:CXC-COMP] (which already owns `ref:`, [CXC-COMP-REF]); see [DD-MENU-CONTENT-BAND] for the open default-vs-coexist question."
- captured-here: "physically authored in [SPEC:CXC-MENU] (this document, §6) because it surfaced in this review pass; `part-of` below names its semantic home, not its filing location."
- see-also: [CXC-COMP-REF], [CXC-COMP-LAYER-ORDER], [DD-MENU-CONTENT-BAND]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]

### [DD-MENU-CONTENT-BAND] Ratified — coexist (interim); always-required is the intended future direction

- decision: whether every screen SVG is required to author the `content-start`/`content-end` sentinel pair ([CXC-MENU-CONTENT-BAND]) going forward (opt-in becomes the DEFAULT, `ref:` deprecated for new screens), or the two conventions COEXIST (an author picks whichever is more convenient per screen, and the derivation supports both).
- chosen: COEXIST (interim) — RATIFIED. The derivation accepts EITHER `ref:`-tagged groups OR a content-band pair, per screen; `ref:` is NOT deprecated by this ratify. This is explicitly a TRANSITION-PERIOD state, not the final resting position — see `future-direction`.
- future-direction: "The intended TRAJECTORY is ALWAYS-REQUIRED — the content-band becomes the sole convention for new screens and `ref:` is deprecated — but that cutover is NOT scheduled by this decision. Coexistence is the deliberate interim state for the transition period; a follow-on decision should set the cutover condition (e.g. a screen-migration threshold or a date) rather than letting 'interim' become permanent by default."
- alternatives: (a) the content-band becomes the sole convention for new screens immediately (fewer per-layer tags to maintain on a complex screen, at the cost of a REQUIRED pair an author must remember to place) — NOT chosen for now, named as the eventual direction in `future-direction`; (b) coexistence — the derivation accepts either `ref:`-tagged groups OR a content-band pair, per screen, with the sentinel pair taking precedence when both are present on one screen — CHOSEN, interim.
- rationale: a hard cutover to opt-in would break every existing `ref:`-tagged screen on the day of the change; coexistence lets new screens adopt the band immediately while existing screens migrate on their own schedule, which is the standard shape for a convention transition.
- invalidating_assumption: that the transition period has a natural end condition. None is set by this decision — a follow-on decision should schedule the always-required cutover once enough screens have migrated to the band; until then, coexistence has no expiry and could drift into becoming the permanent state by default rather than by decision.
- status: decided
- resolved-by: [DEC-CXC-MENU-CONTENT-BAND-COEXIST]
- dsd-source: docs/architecture/DECISIONS-menu-resolver-forks.md
- captured-here: "physically authored in [SPEC:CXC-MENU] (this document, §6); `part-of` below names its semantic home."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]

### [CXC-MENU-SLOT-SIZING] Slot sizing and the two slot kinds it must distinguish

- definition: extends [SPEC:CXC-COMP]'s slot model ([CXC-COMP-SLOT]): a `slot:` group's bounding box IS the bound widget's target rect. When the widget's native aspect ratio differs from the slot bbox's aspect ratio, a FIT-MODE resolves the mismatch — LETTERBOX is the ratified DEFAULT ([DD-MENU-FIT-MODE]): the widget scales uniformly to fit entirely within the bbox, residual space left empty, never cropped, never distorted.
- kinds-of-slot: TWO kinds, distinguished by binding shape. (1) SINGLE-WIDGET slots (`slot:gauge@velocity`) — the widget needs ONE binding handed in from outside; the `@<binding>` segment is REQUIRED, per [CXC-COMP-SLOT]'s SETTLED grammar (`slot:<widget>@<binding>`, split on first `@`, `<widget>` a REGISTERED widget kind — [DD-COMP-SLOT-NAMING], [DEC-CXC-COMP-SLOT-DELIMITER-AT]). (2) SELF-CONTAINED-SCENE drop-ins — a scene carrying its OWN internal bindings (e.g. `engine_thrust_panel` subscribing `throttle/left` and `throttle/right` itself with no external hand-off, [CXC-R-SCENE-GEOMETRY-POLICY]'s `worked-example`) is a REGISTERED WIDGET KIND, `scene` — RATIFIED via [DD-MENU-SLOT-SCENE-GRAMMAR] (Arm A). `slot:scene@<mount-scope>` fits the SETTLED grammar UNCHANGED: `<widget>` = `scene`, `<binding>` = an OPTIONAL mount-scope rather than a single bound value; [CXC-COMP-SLOT]'s `slot:<widget>@<binding>` first-`@`-split rule is NOT amended.
- needs-confirm: "⛔ NEEDS-RENDER-ENGINEER-CONFIRM + NEEDS-GLASS. Two claims are UNVERIFIED here: (1) that a self-contained scene actually composites into an arbitrarily-sized slot rect (nested-scene composition at an arbitrary target size is not yet proven); (2) the ROOT-SCALE-IS-A-NO-OP-ON-BAND-TIER caveat — whether a scene's own root-level scale/geometry-policy declaration ([CXC-R-SCENE-GEOMETRY-POLICY]'s `resizable`/`fixed-size` choice) is INERT once the scene is mounted inside a sized slot, i.e. the slot's fit-mode is the ONLY scale that applies and the scene's own declaration must not double-apply. Neither claim is proven by this node; both need the render engineer plus a glass check before this contract is treated as settled."
- relates-to: [CXC-COMP-SLOT] (extends it), [CXC-R-SCENE-GEOMETRY-POLICY] (the unverified root-scale caveat, and the source of the `engine_thrust_panel` self-contained-scene precedent), [DD-MENU-SLOT-SCENE-GRAMMAR] (the ratified grammar decision kind (2) relies on)
- placement-status: "[PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED]. Extends [SPEC:CXC-COMP]'s slot model; likely home is [SPEC:CXC-COMP] itself, same as [CXC-MENU-CONTENT-BAND]."
- captured-here: "physically authored in [SPEC:CXC-MENU] (this document, §6); `part-of` below names its semantic home."
- see-also: [CXC-COMP-SLOT], [CXC-R-SCENE-GEOMETRY-POLICY], [DD-MENU-FIT-MODE], [DD-MENU-SLOT-SCENE-GRAMMAR]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]

### [DD-MENU-SLOT-SCENE-GRAMMAR] Ratified (Arm A) — how a self-contained-scene slot fits the settled `slot:<widget>@<binding>` grammar

- decision: whether a self-contained-scene drop-in (carrying its own internal bindings, [CXC-MENU-SLOT-SIZING]'s kind (2)) fits the SETTLED slot grammar as a REGISTERED WIDGET KIND with an optional mount-scope binding (Arm A), or requires AMENDING the grammar itself to admit a bare `slot:<scene>` group label with no `@binding` at all (Arm B). Flagged explicitly as amending-settled-[DD-COMP-SLOT-NAMING]/[DEC-CXC-COMP-SLOT-DELIMITER-AT] territory — exactly like [DD-MENU-WALK-AMENDMENT] — and was resolved by ratify, not by silently asserting either arm as fact.
- chosen: ARM A — RATIFIED (David, DSD). A self-contained scene registers as a REGISTERED WIDGET KIND, `scene`. `slot:scene@<mount-scope>` fits the SETTLED grammar UNCHANGED: `<widget>` = `scene`, `<binding>` = an OPTIONAL mount-scope rather than a single bound value. [CXC-COMP-SLOT]'s `slot:<widget>@<binding>` first-`@`-split rule stays exactly as ratified — NO grammar amendment. Arm B is NOT adopted.
- alternatives: "Arm A (CHOSEN, ratified)": register a self-contained scene as a REGISTERED WIDGET KIND (`scene`), so a label like `slot:scene@engine-thrust` fits the SETTLED grammar UNCHANGED — `<widget>` is the registered kind, `<binding>` is an OPTIONAL mount-scope rather than a single bound value. No grammar amendment. "Arm B (NOT chosen)": amend the grammar to admit a bare `slot:<scene>` label with NO `@binding` segment — a genuine amendment to a twice-ratified convention (first-hyphen, then reversed to `@`, [DEC-CXC-COMP-SLOT-DELIMITER-AT]); retained here as the alternative David did not take.
- rationale: Arm A costs nothing against the ratified grammar — registering a new widget kind is already how the grammar extends ([CXC-COMP-WIDGET-KIND]'s existing mechanism); Arm B would have cost a parser/grammar change to a document already ratified twice, for a case Arm A already covers.
- invalidating_assumption: that an optional mount-scope binding is expressive enough for every self-contained-scene case. If some future scene genuinely needs zero binding segment at all (not even an optional one), Arm A cannot express it and this decision would need reopening toward Arm B. Retained as a live watch-item, not a blocker to the ratified decision.
- status: decided
- resolved-by: [DEC-CXC-MENU-SLOT-SCENE-GRAMMAR-ARM-A]
- dsd-source: docs/architecture/DECISIONS-menu-resolver-forks.md
- captured-here: "physically authored in [SPEC:CXC-MENU] (this document, §6); `part-of` below names its semantic home."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]

### [DD-MENU-FIT-MODE] Ratified — slot fit-mode: letterbox default; override mechanism deferred

- decision: which FIT-MODE a mismatched-aspect widget uses inside its slot bbox, and how an app author requests a non-default one.
- chosen: LETTERBOX is the ratified DEFAULT (David decided) — uniform scale to fit within the bbox, residual space empty, never cropped or distorted. The OVERRIDE MECHANISM for a non-default fit-mode is explicitly DEFERRED (David, DSD) — not rejected, not scheduled, deliberately left for a future pass. Letterbox stands UNCHANGED as the only behavior until an override mechanism is separately designed and ratified.
- alternatives: (a) letterbox (the default, chosen); (b) stretch — non-uniform scale to fill the bbox exactly, distorting aspect; (c) reflow — the widget re-lays-out its own internal content to match the bbox's aspect rather than scaling a fixed composition. (b) and (c) remain available as a future EXPLICIT per-slot override; the request mechanism for them is DEFERRED, not designed.
- rationale: letterbox never distorts and never crops — the safest default for an art-directed screen where a wrong aspect should be visibly obvious (empty bars) rather than silently wrong (stretched or cropped). Deferring the override mechanism rather than designing it now avoids speculative grammar for a need that has not yet arisen.
- invalidating_assumption: that no widget kind needs to fill its slot exactly with no letterboxing (e.g. a full-bleed background image) before the override mechanism is eventually designed. If one arises sooner, the deferral should be revisited ahead of its own schedule.
- status: decided
- status-scope: "the DEFAULT ARM (letterbox) is decided and unchanged. The OVERRIDE MECHANISM is now explicitly DEFERRED — itself a decided position (defer, not design-now), not an unresolved open question."
- resolved-by: [DEC-CXC-MENU-FIT-MODE-OVERRIDE-DEFERRED]
- dsd-source: docs/architecture/DECISIONS-menu-resolver-forks.md
- captured-here: "physically authored in [SPEC:CXC-MENU] (this document, §6); `part-of` below names its semantic home."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]

### [CXC-MENU-DIALOG-BASE] Dialog-as-base-capability — how a screen requests a confirmation

- definition: extends [SPEC:CXC-COMP]'s base model ([CXC-COMP-BASE]): the confirmation dialog is a BASE capability, authored ONCE in the base SVG — geometry (the dialog frame, its buttons, its message area) is entirely base-owned, per [CXC-COMP-BASE]'s existing base/mode ownership split and its existing dialog slots (`slot:button@confirm`, `slot:button@abort`, `slot:button@close`, `slot:text@message`). A screen never authors its own dialog layout; it INVOKES the base dialog by raising it WITH A MESSAGE — a dynamic stamped label rendered into the base's `slot:text@message` — while every affordance (confirm/abort/close) is a base slot the screen never redraws.
- requirement: a screen or widget that needs a confirmation MUST invoke the base dialog's raise-with-message capability rather than drawing its own dialog box; the base dialog's geometry MUST NOT be duplicated into any screen or mode surface.
- needs-confirm: "the exact raise-with-message INVOCATION API (what a screen calls, what arguments it passes, whether it is a bus message or a direct call) is TO-CONFIRM — not specified by this node."
- relates-to: [CXC-COMP-BASE] (owns the dialog's existing slots this node's invocation raises)
- placement-status: "[PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED]. Extends [SPEC:CXC-COMP]'s base model; likely home is [SPEC:CXC-COMP] itself, same as [CXC-MENU-CONTENT-BAND]."
- captured-here: "physically authored in [SPEC:CXC-MENU] (this document, §6); `part-of` below names its semantic home."
- see-also: [CXC-COMP-BASE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]

### [CXC-MENU-IRREGULAR-REGION] Irregular screen region — two realization modes and their owners

- definition: an irregular (non-rectangular) screen region has TWO distinct realization modes, with different OWNERS and different reuse consequences — recorded here so the choice and its reuse cost are on the graph, not left implicit in a screen's art. (1) MATTE-CLIP (shape = VIEWPORT / presentation): the content renders into a plain RECT, knowing NOTHING about the irregular boundary; a matte layer ABOVE crops it by overdraw ([CXC-R-SPRITE-CONTAINER]'s verified master-mask compositing — no active clip_rect, masking by LAYERING). The content stays shape-agnostic and reusable across boundary shapes ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]). ENGINE-owned. (2) SHAPED-CONTAINMENT (shape = ARENA / constraint): the content's OWN LOGIC keeps its elements inside the polygon (constrains positions to the boundary). NOT an engine capability — the engine renders given positions, it cannot confine them. ANIMATION/CONTENT-owned, and it must take the shape as INPUT, coupling that content to the specific boundary.
- requirement: a screen author choosing an irregular region MUST record which mode applies and MUST NOT assume the engine can enforce SHAPED-CONTAINMENT — if positions must stay inside a boundary, the content generating those positions owns that constraint, the render does not.
- worked-instance: "DRADIS resolves to MATTE-CLIP, for two reasons: (i) semantics — a tactical scope is a window onto a larger space; a contact reaching the edge is CLIPPED (out of sensor range), it must never bounce off a decorative console edge; (ii) reuse — the Qualia-derived contacts animation must stay shape-agnostic ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]), so `slot:dradis-contacts` is a RECT target and the irregular Colonial boundary is a matte layer ABOVE `dradis2d-animation_layer`, cropping by overdraw exactly as [CXC-R-SPRITE-CONTAINER] specifies — the animation itself never learns the boundary's shape."
- relates-to: [CXC-R-SPRITE-CONTAINER] (the matte-clip mechanism), [CXC-R-SCENE-GEOMETRY-POLICY] (the adjacent geometry-declaration contract), [DX-NARROW-WAIST] (the reuse argument for keeping content shape-agnostic)
- placement-status: "[PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED]. Extends [SPEC:CXC-COMP]'s rendering model; likely home is [SPEC:CXC-COMP] itself, same as [CXC-MENU-CONTENT-BAND]."
- captured-here: "physically authored in [SPEC:CXC-MENU] (this document, §6); `part-of` below names its semantic home."
- see-also: [CXC-R-SPRITE-CONTAINER], [CXC-R-SCENE-GEOMETRY-POLICY], [DX-NARROW-WAIST]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]

