## 7. Acceptance — machine signal + glass gate per surface

### [CXC-MENU-VERIFY] Acceptance — machine signal and glass gate per surface

- definition: each requirement carries a machine signal AND, for a human-perceptible surface, an explicit glass gate ([CXC-COMP-VERIFY] precedent). Rows MENU-C1 through MENU-C7 and MENU-C9 test the GENERIC engine and use neutral fixtures; MENU-C8 tests the BSG INSTANCE specifically and is the one row that legitimately names BSG facts.

| ID | Requirement | verify.machine | verify.human (glass) |
|----|-------------|-----------------|------------------------|
| MENU-C1 | A leaf's default path is derived from the join of its ancestors' slugs, never stored ([CXC-MENU-PATH]) | a test loads a fixture tree, asserts the derived path equals the expected slug-path join for a leaf at depth 3 and a leaf at depth 2 | n/a (structural) |
| MENU-C2 | Slug derivation matches the stated rule, including the trim case ([CXC-MENU-SLUG]) | a parametrized test asserts "FOO BAR"->`foo-bar`, "QUX-WIDGET!!"->`qux-widget`, "  BAZ"->`baz`; the BSG instance table ([CXC-MENU-CIC-INVENTORY]) is asserted separately under MENU-C8 | n/a (structural) |
| MENU-C3 | An `annotation.path` override REPLACES the derived path, never appends to it ([CXC-MENU-ANNOTATION]) | a fixture node with `annotation.path` set asserts `resolve` returns exactly that value, ignoring [CXC-MENU-PATH] entirely | n/a (structural) |
| MENU-C4 | The fallback walk tries the leaf, then each ancestor default innermost-first, before the placeholder ([CXC-MENU-WALK]) | a fixture with a `default.<ext>` at an outer ancestor level asserts a missing leaf resolves to it, not the placeholder; a second fixture with defaults at BOTH the immediate-parent level and a grandparent level asserts the immediate-parent one wins (innermost-first) | on glass: an unbuilt STD screen inside a console with an authored console-level backdrop shows that backdrop, not a bare placeholder |
| MENU-C5 | Exhausting the walk yields the placeholder, never another leaf's asset ([CXC-MENU-WALK], [CXC-COMP-MISSING]) | a fixture with no own path and no ancestor defaults asserts the placeholder marker is returned, and a property test asserts no leaf's `resolve` result ever equals a DIFFERENT leaf's own path | on glass: NAVCON/PLOT unbuilt shows "STD / NAVCON / PLOT — screen not built", never HELM's or another console's art |
| MENU-C6 | Doc->tree lint flags every leaf resolving to nothing queryable ([CXC-MENU-LINT]) | a fixture tree with one un-annotated leaf carrying no file and no ancestor default asserts an `unbuilt-and-undeclared` finding naming that leaf, and asserts a `pure-widget-no-file`/`live-drop-in` leaf with no file raises NO finding | n/a (structural) |
| MENU-C7 | Tree->doc lint flags every asset file with no corresponding live leaf ([CXC-MENU-LINT]) | a fixture asset directory with one file whose slug-path matches no current tree leaf asserts an `orphan` finding naming that file; a rename-without-file-move fixture asserts BOTH directions fire from the one change | n/a (structural) |
| MENU-C8 | BSG's instance resolves and slugs exactly as documented, scoped to STD only ([CXC-MENU-CIC-INVENTORY], [CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE]) | a snapshot test enumerates all 35 STD (console, sub-tab) pairs from the instance YAML and asserts each derived slug + path against the inventory table; a separate assertion confirms the tree contains NO node for DRADIS/EXT/CHRONO | on glass: each of the 35 STD screens, built or not, is reachable via its console/sub-tab tabs and shows either its art or a correctly labeled placeholder |
| MENU-C9 | Lint ERRORs a sibling-slug collision and a reserved-slug (`default`) leaf, never silently resolving one over the other ([CXC-MENU-LINT]) | two fixtures: sibling labels that collide to the same slug assert a `slug-collision` ERROR naming both labels; a leaf labelled to derive `default` asserts a `reserved-slug` ERROR naming that leaf | n/a (structural) |

- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MENU]

---

## 8. Design Decisions

### [DD-MENU-SCHEMA-FORMAT] Menu source format — settled to YAML, superseding the markdown menu

- decision: whether the menu's SOURCE OF TRUTH moves to structured YAML (superseding `cic_menu_structure.md`) or stays markdown with a separate machine-readable projection.
- chosen: YAML as the sole source, feeding the nav-row renderer, this resolver, a scaffolder, and [CXC-MENU-LINT] from one file ([CXC-MENU-SCHEMA], [CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE]'s `source-of-truth-supersession`). SETTLED — David decided.
- migration: the nav-row renderer's `menu.py` parser and BSG's publish step (today reading `cic_menu_structure.md`) MUST be migrated to read the YAML tree instead; `cic_menu_structure.md` is retired once both consumers migrate, not before — until then it remains the human-readable projection, not the source of truth.
- alternatives: (a) keep `cic_menu_structure.md` as the human-edited source and generate/maintain a separate YAML/JSON projection for tooling (rejected: two files invite exactly the drift measured in [CXC-MENU-CIC-INSTANCE]'s `measured-evidence-for-single-source`); (b) embed the tree directly in code as a Python literal (rejected: not editable by a non-engineer, and re-derives the hand-maintained-dict disease this spec exists to retire).
- rationale: a single structured file is the only form four independent consumers (nav renderer, resolver, scaffolder, lint) can share without a translation step that can itself drift; markdown cannot be parsed as a tree without inventing an ad hoc convention on top of it.
- invalidating_assumption: that all four consumers can tolerate YAML specifically (vs., say, a `.lts` menu document parsed by the SAME FactLattice engine this spec is authored in — an alternative not explored here). This is retained as a note for future reconsideration, not a live blocker to the settled decision.
- status: decided
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MENU]

### [DD-MENU-WALK-AMENDMENT] Ratified — the fallback walk amends the never-fall-through rule

- decision: whether [CXC-COMP-MISSING]'s screen-level "missing -> placeholder, straight, no intermediate step" is AMENDED to the ancestor-default walk ([CXC-MENU-WALK]), or the walk is dropped and every miss resolves the placeholder as today.
- chosen: Arm (a) ADOPTED — David ratified the ancestor-default walk via the DSD. [CXC-COMP-MISSING] is now amended by [CXC-MENU-WALK] exactly as specified. The no-op-if-unused degradation is preserved and remains true post-ratify: absent any authored `default.<ext>` anywhere in a tree, resolved behavior stays byte-identical to pre-amendment [CXC-COMP-MISSING].
- alternatives: (a) adopt the walk as specified in [CXC-MENU-WALK] — CHOSEN; (b) keep [CXC-COMP-MISSING] unamended — no walk, ever — NOT chosen, retained here as the historical alternative David considered and did not take.
- rationale: the walk is genuinely useful (graceful degradation for a screen mid-authored) and it is a change to a RATIFIED invariant, so [DX-INTERPRETATION-IS-AN-INFERENCE] governed the process correctly: the amendment was surfaced as a question rather than silently applied, and David has now formally closed it.
- invalidating_assumption: that BSG actually wants any ancestor-level `default.<ext>` authored at all. If none is ever authored, the ratified walk is a no-op in practice; [CXC-MENU-VERIFY]'s MENU-C4 still degrades to [CXC-COMP-MISSING]'s existing test coverage with zero observable behavior change on the BSG instance until a `default.<ext>` is actually authored somewhere.
- edge-on-ratify: see [CXC-MENU-WALK]'s `on-ratify` note — the ratify gate is now CLEAR; the `RELATED_TO` edge to [CXC-COMP-MISSING] still upgrades to a typed `AMENDS` edge only once the SECOND gate (an `EDGETYPE-AMENDS` profile registration) also lands.
- status: decided
- resolved-by: [DEC-CXC-MENU-WALK-AMENDMENT-ADOPTED]
- dsd-source: docs/architecture/DECISIONS-menu-resolver-forks.md
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MENU]

### [DD-MENU-SPEC-HOME] Ratified — new document (option a); section 6 migrates to [SPEC:CXC-COMP] as a follow-on

- decision: whether the generic resolver contract lives as its OWN document (this file, `77-composeux-menu-resolver.lts`) or as a new section inside [SPEC:CXC-COMP].
- chosen: OPTION (a) — a NEW document, this file. RATIFIED (David, DSD). The header's `[PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED]` tag is RESOLVED to `[PLACEMENT RATIFIED]` — see the header and [CXC-MENU-WHY]'s `placement-status`. PLUS a ratified FOLLOW-ON: section 6's [SPEC:CXC-COMP]-adjacent nodes physically MIGRATE into [SPEC:CXC-COMP] in a separate pass — they already carry `part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]` + `captured-here`, so semantic ownership is already correct even while the TEXT stays physically in this file for this landing; the migration does NOT block landing.
- alternatives: (a) this new `77-` document — CHOSEN; (b) fold as new sections into [SPEC:CXC-COMP] (con: [SPEC:CXC-COMP] is already SVG/slot-specific policy — folding a domain-agnostic engine contract into a policy-heavy document is the inverse of the seam [CXC-MENU-WHY] draws, and would make the engine harder to port to a second app without also porting BSG's SVG vocabulary as unwanted baggage); (c) a THIRD corpus root reserved for cross-app generic engines, separate from `platform-specs`' current BSG-heavy tail (not evaluated in depth, not chosen). Retained as the historical alternatives David did not take.
- rationale: keeping engine and instance in ONE document (this file) rather than a fourth split satisfies [DX-NARROW-WAIST] at the DOCUMENT level too — the instance section cites the engine sections by node id, never duplicates them — while a fold into [SPEC:CXC-COMP] would have left no home for a second app's future instance section without re-opening BSG's own spec.
- migration-follow-on: "the §6 PHYSICAL migration to [SPEC:CXC-COMP] is a RATIFIED FOLLOW-ON, not a landing blocker. §6 nodes already carry `part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMP]` + `captured-here` (added ahead of this ratify, during the QA remediation pass), so the graph already asserts their correct semantic home; only the TEXT's physical location moves, in a separate pass, on [SPEC:CXC-COMP]'s own next revision."
- invalidating_assumption: that `platform-specs` numbering 77 stays free at land time — [CXC-VISION-PLACEMENT] already records this directory's history of numeric-prefix collisions (two live pairs as of its writing); this is a check for the LANDING step (git-expert), not a design question, and does not reopen this ratified decision if 77 is still free.
- status: decided
- resolved-by: [DEC-CXC-MENU-SPEC-HOME-NEW-DOC]
- dsd-source: docs/architecture/DECISIONS-menu-resolver-forks.md
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-MENU]

